    <table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
    <tr>
        <td id="descriptionArea">
            <p><b>Defect ID:</b> DEF-004</p>

<p>The Refund API allows multiple refund transactions to be processed for the same settled payment. When the same refund request is submitted more than once, the API creates additional refund transactions instead of preventing duplicate refunds or returning the existing refund record.</p>

<p><b>Preconditions</b></p>

<ol>
	<li>An order has been successfully created via the Orders API.</li>
	<li>The payment has been successfully authorized, captured, and settled.</li>
	<li>The order is eligible for a refund according to business rules.</li>
	<li>Valid authentication credentials/token are available.</li>
	<li>The Refund API endpoint is available and accessible in Postman.</li>
	<li>The Payment ID and Order ID are known and available for testing.</li>
	<li>The user has permission to process refunds.</li>
</ol>


<p><b>Steps to Reproduce</b></p>

<ol>
	<li>Open Postman.</li>
	<li>Retrieve the payment details and verify the payment status is <b>Settled</b>.</li>
	<li>Send a POST request to the Refund endpoint with a valid Payment ID and refund amount.</li>
	<li>Verify that the refund request is processed successfully.</li>
	<li>Submit the exact same refund request again using the same Payment ID and refund amount.</li>
	<li>Retrieve the refund records and transaction history for the payment.</li>
	<li>Observe the refund transactions created by the API.</li>
</ol>


<p><b>Expected Result</b></p>

<ol>
	<li>The first refund request is processed successfully.</li>
	<li>A refund transaction is created and marked as <b>Successful</b>.</li>
	<li>The refunded amount matches the original settled payment amount.</li>
	<li>The order status is updated appropriately.</li>
	<li>The refund is recorded in transaction history.</li>
	<li>When the same refund request is submitted again, the API rejects the request or returns the existing refund record according to business rules.</li>
	<li>No duplicate refund transactions are created.</li>
</ol>


<p><b>Actual Result</b></p>

<ol>
	<li>The initial refund request is processed successfully.</li>
	<li>The same refund request can be submitted multiple times.</li>
	<li>Additional refund transactions are created for the same payment.</li>
	<li>Multiple refunds are processed against the same settled payment.</li>
	<li>Duplicate refund records appear in transaction history.</li>
</ol>


<p><b>Impact</b></p>

<ul>
	<li>Customers may receive multiple refunds for a single payment.</li>
	<li>Financial losses may occur due to over-refunding.</li>
	<li>Transaction history becomes inaccurate.</li>
	<li>Audit and reconciliation processes are compromised.</li>
	<li>Violates idempotency and refund business rules.</li>
</ul>



<p><b>Suggested Fix</b><br/>
Implement idempotency validation and refund eligibility checks to prevent duplicate refunds for the same payment transaction. The API should either reject duplicate requests with an appropriate error response or return the original refund transaction.</p>

<p><b>Severity:</b> Critical</p>
<ul class="alternate" type="square">
	<li>This can directly result in duplicate financial refunds and monetary loss.<br/>
<b>Priority:</b> High</li>
</ul>

            <br/>
        </td>
    </tr>
    </table>

